  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 340 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong to severe thunderstorms are expected this\nthis afternoon and evening, along with the potential for\nlocalized areas of flash flooding through tonight. \n\nA warm front will move north of the Potomac River and the lower\nMD Eastern Shore before daybreak which will set the stage for a\nwarm and muggy Tuesday. A few showers will develop and move\nacross the FA, especially eastern areas, between now and 10 am\nbut the main show comes later this morning into this afternoon\nand evening ahead of a potent upper trough. Flow aloft \nstrengthens considerably by the afternoon hours with 40-50 kt \nof flow noted at 500mb. Low level moisture also ramps up with \nPWAT values climbing back above 2\". Instability is a bit more \nuncertain with clouds possibly hampering heating to some extent \nbut models still show 2000- 3000 J/kg of MLCAPE across the area.\nStorm mode is also somewhat uncertain with the potential for a \nmix of linear segments and semi-discrete cells. All these \nfactors point to a robust severe wx threat across the region. \nSPC has most of the area in a Slight Risk (level 2/5) with a \nsliver of Enhanced Risk (lvl 3/5) clipping far northern portions\nof the MD Eastern Shore. Could see the higher risk area being \nshifted a bit south and east in subsequent outlooks pending \ngreater clarity on degree of cloud cover and storm morphology. \nAs is typical for Mid-Atlantic July severe setups, the main \nthreat will be from damaging winds but a tornado or two cannot \nbe completely ruled out, especially across the N and NE portions\nof the region where the shear and low level helicity are \nprogged to be strongest. The threat of severe storms should last\nwell into the evening hours if not into early Wednesday morning.\nAdditionally, the fast storm motion will largely preclude a\nwidespread flooding risk. However, areas that get repeated\nstorms late today into tonight due have a risk of flash\nflooding (SLGT ERO down to the VA/NC border). \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The threat of severe thunderstorms will linger\ninto Wednesday and the potential for torrential rainfall to\nproduce scattered instances of flash flooding will also\nincrease through Wednesday night. \n\nThe severe threat likely continues into Wednesday, but with the\nhigher probabilities shifted to the south and east ahead of a \nsouthward-moving cold front. How convection evolves on Tuesday \nwill also influence the degree of cloud cover and \ndestabilization potential on Wednesday. That said, the flow \naloft is forecast to remain rather strong. SPC has a Slight Risk\noutlined for our entire CWA (lvl 2/5).\n\nDespite the ongoing drought conditions, Wednesday into Wednesday\nnight is showing an increasingly confident signal for localized\nflooding, especially in urban and poor drainage areas. WPC has \nmost of the area in a Slight Risk ERO again Wednesday with PWAT\ngenerally 2-2.25\". Decent flow aloft should keep individual \ncells moving but training storms on top of the rainfall received\nTuesday could cause scattered instances of flash flooding. All\ntotal, the QPF forecast through Thursday has generally 2-5\" of\nrain from Richmond and Emporia eastward to SE MD and NE NC. \n\nSome drier air finally moves back into the region Thursday and \nFriday but some showers and storms will remain possible, mainly \nacross the southern and southeastern portions of the area.",
